WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:04.179
Let's discuss the features and tools within Sequencer

2
00:00:04.179 --> 00:00:06.860
and how they can impact your speed and efficiency when

3
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.220
using them.

4
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.140
Working our way numerically from one to five,

5
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.580
let's cover the Sequencer interface.

6
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.619
In section one, this is the toolbar,

7
00:00:15.619 --> 00:00:18.280
which is a collection of commonly used tools

8
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.339
and settings within Sequencer.

9
00:00:20.339 --> 00:00:23.699
We have the option to save the sequence,

10
00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:26.500
browse to the sequence within the content browser,

11
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.940
create a new camera and set it as the current camera cut,

12
00:00:31.059 --> 00:00:33.939
export the sequence to a movie or image sequence

13
00:00:33.939 --> 00:00:37.009
via Movie Render Queue, open the Blueprint

14
00:00:37.009 --> 00:00:41.280
Director for the sequence, execute various actions.

15
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.280
Now, note, many of these actions

16
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.390
are available in the interface or with hotkeys.

17
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.070
Change your interface layout and the information

18
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.950
that's displayed, and modify your playback options.

19
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.310
Now, the next three menu options

20
00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.049
are some of the most commonly used

21
00:00:58.049 --> 00:01:00.390
when working with Sequencer.

22
00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.869
The first option gives the ability

23
00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:04.989
to control default keying channels as well as

24
00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:07.290
keyframe interpolation.

25
00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.489
The next option is Auto Keyframe,

26
00:01:09.489 --> 00:01:12.969
which will automatically create a key when parameters

27
00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:14.170
have changed.

28
00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:17.689
A Sequencer will only create a key if a previous key has

29
00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:20.140
been created.

30
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.819
And lastly, we can restrict Sequencer's ability

31
00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:25.340
to automatically create keys for properties

32
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.700
when they're modified within the sequence, the level,

33
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.390
or both.

34
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.230
The Viewport Selectability button

35
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:36.370
limits your ability to choose actors within the Viewport.

36
00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.689
When this button is active, you can only select objects

37
00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:40.780
using the sequence.

38
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.299
You can disable snapping or enable it,

39
00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:46.340
as well as change the snapping interval.

40
00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:49.099
You could modify the frame rate of the sequence.

41
00:01:49.099 --> 00:01:50.980
Finally, you can use the Curve Editor

42
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.000
for granular control over your keyframes.

43
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.799
Section 2 is the Outliner.

44
00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:00.120
Actors and their properties that are part of the sequence

45
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.079
are displayed in the section.

46
00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:05.920
Note, typically only some of the actor's keyable properties

47
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.479
will be shown here when the actor is added to the sequence.

48
00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.360
You can add more keyable properties

49
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.159
using the Add button, the Plus button,

50
00:02:14.159 --> 00:02:18.199
located on the Actors section, or by adding a keyframe

51
00:02:18.199 --> 00:02:23.699
to the actor's properties from a detail panel.

52
00:02:23.699 --> 00:02:25.580
Section 3 is our Timeline.

53
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.379
As the playhead advances, it will progress

54
00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:29.620
through the various keyframes, triggers,

55
00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.750
and events based on their position on the timeline.

56
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.750
Section 4 is the Selection tab, which

57
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.349
can be docked or the default flyout,

58
00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:42.509
and contains many of the same properties displayed

59
00:02:42.509 --> 00:02:46.280
when right-clicking an actor in the sequence.

60
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.039
Lastly, Section 5 contains our playback controls,

61
00:02:50.039 --> 00:02:52.479
buttons to specify our in and out points

62
00:02:52.479 --> 00:02:56.520
for the sequence or shot, and our timeline zoom and view

63
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.830
options.

64
00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.590
Now, Recent Updates to Sequencer

65
00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.710
allows you to filter tracks based

66
00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.870
on your Outliner selections.

67
00:03:05.909 --> 00:03:08.030
You can isolate only the selected tracks,

68
00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:11.389
hide selected tracks, or display all.

69
00:03:11.389 --> 00:03:13.669
After selecting a track or tracks,

70
00:03:13.669 --> 00:03:16.270
the Sequencer Curves Editor can also

71
00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:20.189
be used to manipulate keyframes and keyframe interpolations.

72
00:03:20.189 --> 00:03:23.469
Now, across the top of the Sequencer Curve Editor window

73
00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:27.030
are various features for displaying, manipulating,

74
00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.389
scaling, and snapping your keyframes.

75
00:03:30.389 --> 00:03:32.590
Additionally, you have the ability to set the keyframe

76
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.629
interpolations, as well as the in and out timings

77
00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:38.479
for those keyframes.

78
00:03:38.479 --> 00:03:41.360
The Add Level Sequence with Shots tool

79
00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.560
is useful for automatically generating a master sequence

80
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.280
that includes a structured hierarchy of nested sequences

81
00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.479
and associated sequences, all within a highly organized folder

82
00:03:52.479 --> 00:03:53.819
structure.

83
00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:56.180
While you'll most likely find that this tool doesn't

84
00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.099
work perfectly for your established pipeline

85
00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:02.099
or desired folder structure, I highly

86
00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:04.740
recommend you play with it at least once

87
00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.099
to observe the way Unreal creates the parent

88
00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:10.939
sequence, nested child sequences, and the file

89
00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:13.500
and folder structures.
